          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong convex space
            (also called
            %strong barycentric algebra
            and other terms, invented independently many times) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} equipped
            with a notion of taking weighted averages, or convex-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combinations"}> linear combinations
            , of its elements. Do not confuse this with an (abstract)
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex set
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of convex spaces is the category of algebras of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            = succeed "-(semi)modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Meng"} Meng (1989)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+category"} category of algebras
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitary+monad"} finitary monad
            %a{:href => "#Jacobs10"} Jacobs (2010)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Swirszcz"} Swirszcz
          %p
            The category of convex spaces is is complete, cocomplete, symmetric monoidal closed under the (usual) tensor product construction, and has a cogenerator
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Borger"} Börger and Kemper (1994)
          %p
            Axiomatically, a convex space can be characterized as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equipped with a family of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p : X \times X \to X
            satisfying some natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            (described below).
          %p
            For examples all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonunital+ring"} nonunital
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            are convex spaces, with the map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo −
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,y) = x + p(y-x)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            assigning to any set the free convex space on that set is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitary+monad"} finitary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Jacobs10"} Jacobs (2010)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+ring"} generalized ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            are modules of a field. This is also true of the relatives of convex spaces:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine spaces
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+space"} conical spaces
            %strong affine spaces
            are convex spaces as defined below.
          %p
            Of particular importance are convex spaces parametrized by the interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = [0,1]
            or the Boolean algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = \{0,1\}
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Jacobs09"} Jacobs (2009)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                dual convex space
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/dual+convex+space"} ?
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong convex space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equipped with:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a multiplicatively closed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiring"} (semi)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\in Q
                there exists an element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\in Q
                such that
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mi q
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+q=1
            %li
              %p
                an operation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p: X \times X \to X
                defined for all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\in Q
          %p such that the following identities always hold:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_0(x,y) = x
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,x) = x
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in Q
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo −
                        %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,y) = c_{1-p}(y,x)
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in Q
              (in semiring case replace
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1-p
              by any choice of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \in Q
              that satisfies
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi q
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+q = 1
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x, c_q(y,z)) = c_{p q}(c_r(x,y),z)
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo ,
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p,q,r \in Q
              satisfying
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi p
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(1 - q) = (1 - p q)r
          %p
            As a consequence of the first and third axioms,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1(x,y) = c_0(y,x) = y
          %p
            This defines convex spaces as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety+of+algebras"} variety of algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            The intended interpretation is that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo −
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi p
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,y) = x + p(y-x) = (1-p)x + p y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,y)
            is the
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            gets weight
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1-p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            gets weight
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            as a continuous parameter, this interpretation has the advantage of “starting” at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            at “rate”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            This interpretation is ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bias"}> biased
            ’, in the sense that the centered choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p=0
            favors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bias"} unbiased
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p := (p_1,\ldots,p_n)
            satisfying
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i = 1}^n p_i = 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x := (x^1,\ldots, x^n)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x) := \sum_i p_i x^i
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of convex spaces may be called a
            %strong convex-linear map
            or an
            %strong affine linear map
            (since an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            is a convex space with extra properties, as in the examples below). It should probably
            %em not
            be called a ‘convex map’, which (between affine spaces) is something more general.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Any real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            is a convex space, with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo −
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,y) = x + p(y-x)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combination"} linear combination
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
          %p
            More generally, any real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            is a convex space; since
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p + (1 - p) = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p
            in a vector space is valid in an affine space. In the unbiased version, any convex-linear combination is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+linear+combination"} affine linear combination
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+properties"} extra properties
          %p
            Still more generally, any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+subset"} convex subset
            (that is, one containing the entire line segment between two given points) of a real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            is a convex space (again with extra properties, which are described algebraically below).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+field"} Boolean field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}
            is a convex space with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,y) = x \vee y = x + y - x y
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi p
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt p \lt 1
            (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_0(x,y) = x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1(x,y) = y
            as always); this cannot be realised as a subset of a vector space. This can be generalised to any (possibly unbounded)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice"} semilattice
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %h2#abstract_convex_sets Abstract convex sets
          %p
            There is a nice abstract converse to the example of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+subset"} convex subset
            of an affine space. A convex space is
            %strong cancellative
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = z
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,y) = c_p(x,z)
            for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \ne 0
            = succeed "." do
              %strong abstract convex set
          .un_theorem
            %p
              %strong Theorem
              (Thm. 2 in the paper by Stone)
            %p A convex space is cancellative if and only if it is isomorphic (as a convex space) to a convex subset of some real affine space.
          %p
            Compare this with the theorem that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cancellative+monoid"} cancellative
            if and only if it is isomorphic to a submonoid of some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
          %p
            Of course, most of the examples given above are cancellative, being manifestly given as convex subsets of real affine space. However, the last example — a semilattice with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p(x,y) = x \vee y
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi p
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt p \lt 1
            — is non-cancellative.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+convex+space"} super convex space
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+function"} convex function
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+monad"} distribution monad
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+of+probability%2C+measures%2C+and+valuations"} monads of probability, measures, and valuations
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"}> vector space
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+space"} conical space
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Convex spaces have been rediscovered many times under many different names. References tend to define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_p
            only for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi p
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt p \lt 1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Analysis+and+its+Foundations"} Handbook of Analysis and its Foundations
                , Section 12.7 (short and to the point).
            %li#Borger
              %p Börger &amp; Kemper, Cogenerators for convex spaces, Applied Categorical Structures, Vol. 2 (1994), 1-11.
            %li
              %p
                Romanowska, Smith, Orłowska; Abstract barycentric algebras;
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://staff.science.uva.nl/~gfontain/tacl09-abstracts/tacl2009_submission_48.pdf"} pdf
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
                to an arbitrary
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mi Π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \Pi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                for ‘Łukasiewicz’,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                for ‘product’, so think of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
                as a space of fuzzy truth values).
            %li
              %p Romanowska &amp; Smith (1985); Modal Theory: An Algebraic Approach to Order, Geometry, and Convexity; Res. Exp. Math. 9; Heldermann-Verlag, Berlin, 1985.
            %li
              %p
                Marshall Harvey Stone,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Postulates for the barycentric calculus
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Fritz"}> Tobias Fritz
                , Convex spaces I: definition and examples.
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0903.5522"} arXiv/0903.5522
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Fritz"} Tobias Fritz
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"} Tom Leinster
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/johnbaez/show/Convex+spaces+and+an+operadic+approach+to+entropy"} Convex spaces and an operadic approach to entropy
                = succeed "Lab" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li#Jacobs09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"}> Bart Jacobs
                ,
                %em Duality for convexity
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.3834"} arXiv/0911.3834
            %li#Jacobs10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"}> Bart Jacobs
                , Convexity, duality and effects. In: IFIP International Conference on Theoretical Computer Science. Springer. 2010, pp. 1–19. (
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-15240-5_1.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p J.R. Isbell, Adequate subcategories, Illinois Journal of Math, 4, 541-552 (1960).
            %li
              %p
                Shiri Artstein-Avidan, Vitali Milman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The concept of duality in convex analysis, and the characterization of the Legendre transform
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 169
            %li
              %p
                Joe Flood,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Semiconvex geometry
                %strong 30
                (1980/81), 496-–510.
            %li#Swirszcz
              %p
                T. Swirszcz,
                %em Monadic functors and categories of convex sets
                , Preprint No.
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 70
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Proc. Inst. Math. Pol. Acad. Sci.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Monadic functors and convexity
                %em Bull. Acad. Polon. Sci. Ser. Sci. Math. Astronom. Phys.
                %strong 22
                (1974), 39–42.
            %li
              %p
                Stanley P. Gudder,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Convexity and mixtures
                %strong 19
                (1977), 221–240;
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A general theory of convexity
                %strong 49
                (1979), 89–96.
            %li#Meng
              %p
                Xiao-qing Meng,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories of convex sets and of metric spaces with applications to stochastic programming and related areas
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Meng.djvu", :title => "Ph.D. thesis of Meng"} djvu
          %p
            Many other references, and a discussion of how convex spaces have been repeatedly rediscovered, can be found at the
            = succeed "-Category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/04/convex_spaces.html"} Convex Spaces
